புதிய உலகத்துக்கு ஒரு பெயர் வேண்டும். இங்கிலாந்தின் ஆக்ஸ்ஃபோர்டு நகரில் வசித்த 11 வயதுச் சிறுமி வெனீஷியா பர்னி, ‘புளூட்டோ’ என்ற பெயரைச் சொன்னாள். ரோமானியக் கதைகளில் புளூட்டோ பாதாள உலகின் கடவுள். இருண்ட, குளிர்ந்த ஓர் இடத்துக்கு இதைவிடப் பொருத்தமான பெயர் ஏது? அவளுடைய தாத்தா இந்த யோசனையை ஒரு வானியலாளரிடம் சொன்னார். வானாய்வகமும் அதையே தேர்ந்தெடுத்தது.

பெயரின் முதல் இரண்டு ஆங்கில எழுத்துகள் P, L. வானாய்வகத்தை நிறுவிய பெர்சிவல் லோவெலின் பெயரின் முதல் எழுத்துகளும் அவையே. ஒன்பதாவது கோளைத் தேடுவதில் பல ஆண்டுகளைச் செலவிட்டவர் அவர்.

{{story:names-meaning}}

The German [[botanist|botanist]] Wilhelm Barthlott studied plant leaves under a powerful [[electron|electron]] microscope in the 1970s. He noticed that the lotus, and some other plants, stayed clean however dirty their surroundings were. In 1997 he and a colleague explained why, and named it the lotus effect.

Companies have since copied it. There are self-cleaning paints for buildings that shed rain and grime, coatings for glass and fabrics that repel stains. Designers love borrowing ideas from nature like this, a habit you can read about in [[@design-everyday|design]].

{{story:design-everyday}}

For very long gaps, engineers hang the road from cables. In a suspension bridge, two thick main cables are draped over tall towers and anchored at each end. Thinner cables drop down to hold the road. [[@golden-gate|The Golden Gate Bridge]] in [[@san-francisco|San Francisco]], opened in 1937, has a main span of 1,280 metres, longer than a dozen football fields laid end to end.

Cable-stayed bridges run straight cables from the towers directly to the road, in a fan or harp pattern. [[@mumbai|Mumbai]]'s [[@india-wonder-bandra-sealink|Bandra–Worli Sea Link]], opened in 2009, is one, stretching out over the Arabian Sea.

{{story:shapes-angles}}

Rahman built a recording studio next to his home and called it Panchathan Record Inn. There he composed hundreds of [[@advertising|advertising]] jingles, learning to say [[@behind-theme-music|something catchy]] in thirty seconds.

He worked mostly at night and treated the studio like an instrument. He recorded parts one at a time and layered them, the way a painter adds coats of colour. His method meant he could spend weeks on a single song, adjusting tiny details until it felt right.

{{story:sound-recording}}

The Commission was set up on 25 January 1950, a day before India became a [[republic|republic]]. That date is now marked as National Voters' Day. Its first chief, Sukumar Sen, a mathematician by training, had to organise the first general election for about 17 crore voters, most of whom had never voted before.

His team made voters' lists by going house to house, designed ballot boxes and party symbols, and trained lakhs of officials. When many women gave their names only as someone's wife or mother, he insisted they be recorded with their own names. Sen later helped run Sudan's first elections too.

{{story:democracy-india}}

In 1938, researchers at Harvard University began following a group of young men: students at the university and boys from poor Boston neighbourhoods. They asked about their health, work and families, and kept asking for decades. Later, the study added their wives and children.

When the men were in their 80s, researchers looked back at who had stayed healthy and happy. The strongest clue wasn't wealth, cholesterol or career success. It was how satisfied people had been with their relationships at age 50. The current director, psychiatrist Robert Waldinger, sums it up simply: good relationships keep us happier and healthier.

{{story:gratitude}}

Experience is measured in flying hours. In the USA, the Federal Aviation Administration says a pilot usually needs at least 1,500 hours before flying for an airline as a first officer, the co-pilot who sits on the right. Many young pilots build those hours by teaching other students.

Training is expensive. A commercial licence can cost tens of lakhs of rupees in India and tens of thousands of dollars in the USA. Some airlines run cadet schemes that pay for training in return for a promise to work for them. Another path is through the [[@indian-air-force|Indian Air Force]] or the US military, where the armed forces train their own pilots.

{{story:wright-brothers}}

Ideas for laws come from many places: a minister's plan, a court ruling, a public campaign or a news story. Most bills are written by government ministries, with lawyers checking the wording.

A bill is introduced in one house, which is called the first reading. In the second reading, members debate it in detail. It may go to a committee of MPs from different parties who study it, invite experts and suggest changes. In the third reading, the house votes. Then the bill goes to the other house to do it all again.

{{story:democracy-athens}}

Most people don't have an identical twin, so transplants needed a way to quiet the [[immune-system|immune system]]. In the 1960s and 1970s, scientists developed medicines that did this. A drug called cyclosporine, which came into wide use in the early 1980s, made transplants far more successful.

Doctors also check how well donor and patient match. First comes blood group. Then they compare tissue markers, [[protein|proteins]] on the surface of cells that act like a name badge. The closer the match, the less the immune system complains. Even so, almost every transplant patient takes anti-rejection medicine for life.

{{story:history-of-medicine}}

Darius I, who ruled from 522 to 486 BCE, organised the empire. He set taxes for each province, introduced gold coins and built Persepolis, a palace city with stone staircases carved with people from across the empire bringing gifts.

He also improved the Royal Road, which ran about 2,500 kilometres from Sardis, in Turkey, to Susa, in Iran. Stations along it kept fresh horses and riders. The Greek writer Herodotus admired these messengers, saying that neither snow, nor rain, nor heat, nor gloom of night stopped them. Those words, slightly adapted, are carved on the James A. Farley Post Office building in [[@new-york|New York]].

{{story:roman-roads}}

Washington suffered from bad teeth for most of his life, and by the time he became president he had only one of his own left. He wore dentures that hurt and changed the shape of his face in his portraits.

{{story:teeth}}

The capital city, [[@washington-dc|Washington, D.C.]], is named after him. The Washington Monument there, a stone obelisk about 169 m tall, was the tallest structure in the world when it was finished in 1884, until the [[@eiffel-tower|Eiffel Tower]] beat it in 1889. His face is also carved on [[@mount-rushmore|Mount Rushmore]], and it appears on the US one-dollar note and the quarter.

Ahilyabai Holkar became ruler of the Maratha state of Malwa, in today's Madhya Pradesh, in 1767, after the deaths of her husband and son. She governed for nearly 30 years from Maheshwar, on the Narmada river.

She was known for fair justice and careful use of money. She built and repaired temples, wells, ghats and rest houses across India, from the Himalayas to the far south. In 1780 she rebuilt the Kashi Vishwanath temple in [[@varanasi|Varanasi]], the version that still stands today.

{{story:school-history}}

Sandstone is soft enough to cut with iron tools, but strong enough to hold its shape for thousands of years. Workers likely began by cutting steps up the side of the cliff. Then they flattened the top of the area they wanted to carve and worked down, layer by layer.

The proof is in unfinished tombs. Some have neatly carved tops and rough rock at the bottom where work stopped. The most famous building, the Treasury, has a front about 40 m tall, roughly as high as a 13-storey building. Its columns, statues and great stone urn were all carved from one rock face.

{{story:lost-cities}}

The journey has no start or finish, but January is a good place to begin. The herds gather on the short-grass plains of the southern Serengeti, where rain brings rich new grass. From late January to March, hundreds of thousands of calves are born in a few weeks. So many arrive at once that hunters can't catch more than a small share.

As the plains dry out around May, the herds head west and north. Lines of animals stretch for kilometres. By July they reach the Mara River on the border with Kenya, where they spend the dry months. When the short rains return in November, they turn south again.

{{story:animal-migration-land}}

Millets are tough. Pearl millet can grow in the sandy soils of Rajasthan where rice would die. Many millets need far less water than rice and ripen quickly, sometimes in under three months.

That matters more as weather becomes harder to predict. In a year when [[@monsoon|the monsoon]] arrives late, a field of bajra can still give a harvest while thirstier crops fail. Many millets also need little [[fertiliser|fertiliser]], so they suit small farmers who cannot spend much.

{{story:droughts}}

High in the Andes, nights are freezing and days are sunny and dry. The Inca and earlier peoples used this to make chuño. Potatoes were left out to freeze at night and thaw by day, then trampled underfoot to squeeze out water. After a few weeks, they became light, hard and could be stored for years.

Chuño fed armies and helped the [[@inca-machu-picchu|Inca]] store food for bad harvests. It is one of the oldest freeze-dried foods in the world, a method still used in Peru and Bolivia.

{{story:inca-machu-picchu}}
